PAPER 3 (NeurIPS) — FINAL EXPERIMENT SPECIFICATION (PLOT 1–4 + SANITY CHECK)
Version: 2026-02-18
Goal: Produce conclusive, reproducible evidence for the claim:
  “Robust wired CfC architectures can be efficiently plucked from the WS-Flex population using training-free graph proxy metrics
   that are extremely cheap to evaluate relative to training and robustness sweeps.”

CRITICAL ALIGNMENT REQUIREMENT (FIRST-PASS PIPELINE)
This spec MUST match the first-pass architecture_refinement procedure:
  • Generator search space includes: H (units), k_degree, p_rewiring, target_clustering C*, target_path_length L*, and a deterministic seed.
  • Proxy objectives:
      TE proxy = degree entropy (NOT adjacency spectral entropy), normalized by /5.0 and clipped to [0,1]
      ORC proxy = |mean Ollivier–Ricci curvature|, normalized by /10.0 and clipped to [0,1]
  • Connectivity constraint: disconnected graphs are rejected (do not update optimizer / dataset)
  • Graph-to-CfC conversion uses BIDIRECTIONAL wiring:
      For each undirected edge (u,v): set A[u,v]=1 and A[v,u]=1 (diagonal zero)
  • Seed handling: s_g is not optimized; it is deterministic per trial (e.g., trial.number)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0) MOTIVATION, CONTEXT, AND WHY WE PIVOTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem:
  Training and robustness evaluation for recurrent models is expensive. For each candidate topology, we must:
    (i) train to convergence (or early-stop), and
    (ii) sweep perturbation intensities across multiple seeds.
  Sample-and-train enumeration (prior graph-performance correlation studies) becomes impractical for wired CfC.

Limitations of prior attempts (internal):
  Earlier framing emphasized “TPE beats Random” in constrained graph space. Empirically, TPE did not reliably improve extreme-value discovery
  in proxy space under equal budgets; variance and selection instability made it difficult to claim a stronger sampler is the key novelty.

Reframed contribution:
  The novelty is not the specific sampler, but the compute-aware two-stage procedure:
    Stage A: training-free proxy mapping and screening over WS-Flex graphs
    Stage B: train/evaluate only a small subset that is proxy-extreme and proxy-diverse

This spec defines 3 experiments (Plot 1–3) that directly support:
  Claim 1: Proxy mapping is cheap and reveals a broad structured landscape (Plot 1)
  Claim 2: Proxy-selected topologies yield better robustness after training under matched protocol than uniform selection and standard baselines (Plot 2)
  Claim 3: Proxies organize trained robustness/accuracy into interpretable regions and improve “hit-rate” into robust regimes (Plot 3)
Plus a sanity-check: extreme “best proxy” vs “worst proxy” topology (Best/Worst test)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) COMMON DEFINITIONS (EQUATIONS) — USED IN ALL EXPERIMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.1 Graph generator and constraints
  Candidate graph G_u = (V,E), undirected, |V| = H.

  Generator parameters (first-pass aligned):
    θ = (H, k, p, C*, L*, s_g)
    H ∈ [10,128]  (units)
    k ∈ {2,4,6,...,24}, k < H  (even)
    p ∈ [0,1]  (p_rewiring)
    C* ∈ [0.01, 1.0] (target clustering)
    L* ∈ [1.0, 4.5] (target path length)
    s_g deterministic per trial (NOT optimized)

  Connectivity constraint:
    If G_u is disconnected, reject and resample (no proxy record, or record separately as “rejected”; must not update the sampler).

1.2 Graph-to-CfC adjacency mask (bidirectional conversion)
  For each undirected edge (u,v) ∈ E with indices i,j:
    A_ij = 1 and A_ji = 1
  A_ii = 0 (diagonal zero)
  Active recurrent edges:
    E_active = Σ_{i,j} A_ij = 2|E|  (given bidirectional conversion)

1.3 Proxy metrics (first-pass aligned)
  Degree-entropy proxy (TE):
    Let d(v) be degree of node v, and π(m) the empirical distribution over degree values m.
      TE(G) = - Σ_m π(m) log π(m)
      \hat{TE}(G) = clip( TE(G) / 5.0 , 0, 1 )

  Ollivier–Ricci curvature proxy (ORC):
    Mean edge curvature:
      ORC(G) = (1/|E|) Σ_{(x,y)∈E} κ(x,y)
    Proxy uses magnitude only:
      \widehat{|ORC|}(G) = clip( |ORC(G)| / 10.0 , 0, 1 )
    IMPORTANT: Signed ORC is NOT optimized (may be analyzed post-hoc only, clearly labeled).

  Two-dimensional proxy objective:
    f(G) = ( \hat{TE}(G), \widehat{|ORC|}(G) )

  Scalar proxy score (only for “best vs worst” sanity check; NOT a training objective):
    s(G) = 0.5 * ( \hat{TE}(G) + \widehat{|ORC|}(G) )

1.4 Robustness evaluation metric (trained models)
  Let a_t be performance (ROC-AUC) under drift intensity level t, and a_0 clean performance.
  Relative performance curve:
    r_t = a_t / a_0
  Maximum relative degradation:
    RD_max = max_t ( (a_0 - a_t) / a_0 ) = max_t (1 - r_t)

  (Optional additional summary if already in codebase: AUPC / area-under-performance curve; use only if implemented consistently.)

1.5 Perturbation model (AR(1) drift)
  ε_t = ρ ε_{t-1} + η_t,   η_t ~ N(0, σ^2)
  Input perturbation: x_t + ε_t
  Drift intensity grid: pre-defined discrete levels; must be identical for all trained models.

