5) EXPERIMENT 2 (PLOT 2): PROXY-BASED “PLUCKING” IMPROVES TRAINED ROBUSTNESS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Motivation:
  This is the central NeurIPS claim: proxy screening concentrates expensive training on topologies that tend to be more robust
  than uniform selection and standard baselines, under matched training.

Core question:
  Under identical training and robustness protocols, do proxy-selected WS-Flex topologies yield lower RD_max (and better r_t curves)
  than (i) uniform WS-Flex selection and (ii) dense/random-sparse/NCP baselines?

Groups (all trained/evaluated under identical protocol):
  G1: Proxy-selected WS-Flex (K topologies; selected from Experiment 1’s proxy pool using Pareto+diversity)
  G2: Uniform-selected WS-Flex (K topologies; sampled uniformly from the same admissible generator space; must be independent of G1)
  G3: Dense CfC baseline (S seeds)
  G4: Random sparse CfC baseline, sparsity matched (S seeds)
  G5: NCP wiring baseline (S seeds)

Critical control:
  Fix H for all (recommended for main plot). If your code currently varies H, lock H for Plot 2.

Procedure:
  E2.1 Train models:
    For each topology in G1 and G2:
      For each seed s in 1..S:
        - Instantiate wired CfC using bidirectional mask A (E_active tracked).
        - Train under locked protocol; log best checkpoint and final test metrics.
    For baselines:
      - Train Dense CfC with same training protocol for S seeds.
      - Train NCP wiring with same protocol for S seeds.
      - Train Random sparse CfC:
          Option A (preferred): for each WS-Flex topology, generate a random mask with same E_active and train those (pairs).
          Option B: match average E_active of G1 and train S seeds (weaker control; only if code complexity/time is prohibitive).

  E2.2 Robustness evaluation:
    For each trained run:
      - Evaluate clean a_0.
      - Evaluate a_t across drift intensity grid.
      - Compute r_t = a_t/a_0 and RD_max.

Plot output (Plot 2 specification):
  One panel (with optional inset):
    - x-axis: drift intensity index t (ordered)
    - y-axis: relative performance r_t = a_t/a_0
    - Curves: mean across runs for each group (G1..G5)
    - Uncertainty: 95% CI band (bootstrap)
  Optional inset (or second small panel if space):
    - Distribution of RD_max per group (violin/box) + CI

Statistical reporting (minimal but defensible):
  - Report group means and 95% CI for RD_max.
  - Report a simple nonparametric test or effect size:
      Option A: Cliff’s delta between G1 and G2; and between G1 and Dense/Random-sparse.
      Option B: bootstrap difference in means with CI (recommended: simple and aligned with CI bands).
  - Do not overclaim deterministic prediction; claim is improved selection efficiency / hit-rate.

Expected results / hypotheses:
  H3: Proxy-selected WS-Flex (G1) yields lower RD_max than uniform WS-Flex (G2).
  H4: Proxy-selected WS-Flex is competitive with or better than dense/random-sparse CfC and NCP under RD_max, without collapsing clean AUC.

Go/No-go checks:
  - If G1 ≈ G2 on RD_max, main claim “pluck via proxies” fails; pivot to “topology matters” only (we want to avoid this).
    Mitigation if this happens:
      • Increase K modestly (e.g., 12→16) if compute allows.
      • Strengthen diversity selection on Pareto set (avoid selecting many similar points).
      • Verify that proxies and selection are computed correctly (TE definition, ORC magnitude, clipping).
  - If random sparse baseline is not properly matched, reviewers will challenge results. Ensure matching is logged.

Reuse vs updates:
  Reuse:
    - Existing training pipeline, dataset loading, drift perturbation evaluation, RD_max computation (if already used).
  Update:
    - Add “uniform-selected WS-Flex” trained set if not already present.
    - Ensure random sparse baseline truly matches E_active (preferred).
    - Ensure plotting uses relative performance r_t for comparability.

