7) SANITY CHECK (BEST VS WORST PROXY EXTREMES) — MUST RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Motivation:
  A simple, reviewer-friendly “does the proxy mean anything at all?” check.
  This reduces risk: if it fails, you learn immediately and can adjust selection messaging.

Design:
  From the SAME proxy pool used in Experiment 1 (same H, same bounds):
    - Best proxy graph: argmax s(G) where s(G)=0.5*(\hat{TE} + \widehat{|ORC|})
    - Worst proxy graph: argmin s(G)

Training/eval:
  Train both topologies for S seeds under the same protocol.
  Evaluate robustness curves and RD_max.

Output:
  Option A (appendix figure): Two curves best vs worst with 95% CI band.
  Option B (main inset): If separation is large and stable.

Expected results:
  H7: Best-proxy topology shows better robustness curve / lower RD_max than worst-proxy topology.

Go/No-go:
  - If worst consistently beats best, do NOT claim scalar proxy “predicts robustness.”
    Instead, emphasize Pareto+diversity selection as an exploration tool (proxies as coordinates), and keep sanity check in appendix with discussion.

Reuse vs updates:
  Reuse:
    - Same training and robustness eval pipeline.
  Update:
    - Add “extreme selector” that picks argmax/argmin s(G) from proxy dataset.

