11) IMPLEMENTATION REFERENCE (architecture_refinement/paper3)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This section documents the concrete implementation in architecture_refinement/paper3.
All scripts and functions are named for reference; the description is in plain English.

11.1 Master script and run order
  The entry point is run_all_paper3_plots.py. It executes in strict order:
    1. Experiment 1 (proxy collection)
    2. Sanity Check (best vs worst)
    3. Experiment 2 (train G1..G5)
    4. Experiment 3 (aggregation and analysis)
    5. Plot generation (Plots 1–4)
  Each step can be skipped via flags (--skip-exp1, --skip-sanity, etc.).

  Default output root: architecture_refinement/outputs/paper3
  Subdirectories: experiment1/, sanity_check/, experiment2/, experiment3/
  Plots: plot1_proxy_landscape.pdf, plot2_robustness_curves.pdf, plot3_proxy_plane.pdf, plot4_architecture_comparison.pdf

11.2 Proxy metric computation
  All proxy values are computed by architecture_refinement.metrics_te_orc.compute_paper3_proxies.
  It takes a networkx graph and returns (te_hat, orc_hat):
    - Degree entropy (Shannon entropy of the empirical degree distribution) is computed, divided by 5.0, and clipped to [0,1].
    - Ollivier–Ricci curvature is computed as the mean over edges, the magnitude is taken, divided by 10.0, and clipped to [0,1].
  The graph generator is architecture_refinement.ws_flex_generator.build_plain_ws_flex (Watts–Strogatz ring lattice).

11.3 Experiment 1 (run_paper3_experiment1.py)
  Run: python -m architecture_refinement.paper3.run_paper3_experiment1 --output-dir <path>

  Procedure:
    - Samples N_proxy graphs (default 500) from the parameter space: k ∈ {2,4,...,24}, p ∈ [0,1], graph_seed random.
    - For each trial, the graph is built with seed = trial (deterministic per trial).
    - Disconnected graphs are rejected; the loop continues until N_proxy connected graphs are collected.
    - For each accepted graph, compute_paper3_proxies is called and the result is stored.
    - Pareto front is extracted in the (te_hat, orc_hat) plane (max-max objective).
    - K graphs (default 12) are selected from the Pareto set using diversity: a 6×6 grid bins the proxy plane; bins are sorted by occupancy; one graph is drawn per bin until K are selected.
    - Selected architectures are written as JSON files in selected_architectures/ with schema_version 2, including hidden_adj_undirected for bidirectional wiring.

  Outputs:
    - proxy_pool.csv: all proxy-evaluated graphs with H, k, p, graph_seed, te_hat, orc_hat, n_edges, E_active, runtime_sec.
    - selected_proxy.csv: the K selected subset.
    - selected_architectures/*.json: one JSON per selected topology.
    - experiment1_summary.json: n_proxy, rejected_count, pareto_size, K_selected, mean_proxy_time_sec.

11.4 Sanity Check (run_paper3_sanity_check.py)
  Run: python -m architecture_refinement.paper3.run_paper3_sanity_check --experiment1-dir <path> --output-dir <path>

  Procedure:
    - Loads proxy_pool.csv from Experiment 1.
    - Computes scalar score s = 0.5 * (te_hat + orc_hat) for each row.
    - Best graph: row with maximum s.
    - Worst graph: row with minimum s.
    - Builds both architectures from (k, p, graph_seed) and writes them to sanity_check_pilot/selected_architectures.
    - For each of the two models and S seeds (default 5), invokes the unified experiment runner in test_perturb mode.
    - Collects test_perturb results (clean_roc_auc, RD_max, intensity-level r_t) from the results directory.
    - Aggregates mean RD_max and 95% bootstrap CI.

  Outputs:
    - sanity_check_manifest.json: best/worst model names, te_hat, orc_hat, s.
    - sanity_check_summary.json: best_RD_max_mean, worst_RD_max_mean, 95% bootstrap CIs.
    - sanity_check_curves.csv: intensity-level r_t for plotting.

11.5 Experiment 2 (run_paper3_experiment2.py)
  Run: python -m architecture_refinement.paper3.run_paper3_experiment2 --experiment1-dir <path> --output-dir <path>

  Procedure:
    - G1: Copies the K proxy-selected architectures from Experiment 1 into experiment2_pilot/selected_architectures.
    - G2: Samples K new WS-Flex graphs uniformly (k ∈ {2,...,24}, p ∈ [0,1], independent graph_seed). Uses a fixed RNG seed (202607) distinct from Experiment 1.
    - G3: Creates a dense CfC baseline (complete graph on H nodes).
    - G4: Creates a random sparse CfC baseline with E_active matching the average E_active of G1.
    - G5: Creates an NCP wiring baseline (ncp_autoncp, units=H, sparsity=0.5).
    - Writes experiment2_manifest.json with group names and model lists.
    - For each (model, seed) in G1..G5, invokes evaluation.unified_experiment_runner.py in test_perturb mode.
    - The unified runner trains on clean data, then evaluates robustness by sweeping AR(1) drift intensities across a grid.

  Training and robustness:
    - Mode: test_perturb.
    - Dataset: BNCI2014_001 motor imagery.
    - Eval mode: CrossSession.
    - Perturbation: ar1_drift.
    - Intensity grid: 20 steps from saturation file; alpha grid [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0] for correlated noise.
    - Seeds: 42, 43, ..., 42+S-1.

  Outputs:
    - experiment2_manifest.json: H, K, S, dataset, groups (G1..G5 model lists).
    - experiment2_pilot/selected_architectures/*.json: all architectures.
    - Results are written by the unified runner to results/MotorImagery/BNCI2014_001/<model>/CrossSessionEvaluation/<seed>/.../test_perturb/*.csv.

11.6 Experiment 3 (run_paper3_experiment3.py)
  Run: python -m architecture_refinement.paper3.run_paper3_experiment3 --experiment2-dir <path> --experiment1-dir <path> --output-dir <path>

  Procedure:
    - Loads experiment2_manifest.json to obtain model lists for G1..G5.
    - For each model in G1..G5 and each seed, scans the results directory for test_perturb CSV files.
    - Extracts clean_roc_auc and RD_max for each run. RD_max = max_t(1 - r_t) where r_t = corrupted_roc_auc / clean_roc_auc.
    - For G1 and G2, proxy values (te_hat, orc_hat) are read from architecture JSONs. If missing (e.g. G2 uniform), they are computed on-the-fly from the graph using compute_paper3_proxies.
    - G3, G4, G5 have no proxy coordinates; they are included for the architecture comparison plot.
    - Hit-rate: "robust" = RD_max in the lowest 20% among WS-Flex runs (G1 + G2 only). Computes hit_rate_proxy = P(robust | G1) and hit_rate_uniform = P(robust | G2) with bootstrap CI for the difference.
    - Binned proxy-plane aggregation: 6×6 grid over (te_hat, orc_hat); mean RD_max per bin.

  Outputs:
    - experiment3_results.csv: model, group, seed, te_hat, orc_hat, clean_roc_auc, RD_max for all runs.
    - experiment3_summary.json: hit_rate_proxy, hit_rate_uniform, hit_rate_diff, hit_rate_diff_ci, proxy_plane_bin_means.

11.7 Plotting (plotting.py)
  All plots are saved as PDF at 300 dpi.

  Plot 1 (plot1): Proxy landscape scatter (Panel A), time bars (Panel B), and FLOPS bars (Panel C).
    - Inputs: proxy_pool.csv, selected_proxy.csv, experiment1_summary.json.
    - Panel A: scatter of all proxy points in (te_hat, orc_hat); selected K highlighted with circles and labels S1..SK; axis limits data-driven with y-axis capped at 0.05.
    - Panel B: bar chart of proxy eval time vs training+robustness time (log scale).
    - Panel C: bar chart of estimated FLOPS for proxy vs full eval (log scale). Uses mean_proxy_flops_est, mean_full_flops_est from summary if present; else defaults ~1e7 (proxy) and ~1e12 (full).

  Plot 2 (plot2): Robustness curves and performance–robustness scatter.
    - Inputs: experiment3_results.csv, experiment2_manifest.json.
    - Panel A: r_t curves (intensity vs r_t) for G1 and G2 from intensity-level test_perturb data; mean and 95% bootstrap CI.
    - Panel B: scatter of clean ROC-AUC vs RD_max for G1 and G2; labeled "Performance vs robustness".

  Plot 3 (plot3): Proxy plane and performance–proxy score scatter.
    - Inputs: experiment3_results.csv.
    - Panel A: scatter of (te_hat, orc_hat) colored by RD_max for G1 and G2.
    - Panel B: scatter of clean ROC-AUC vs scalar proxy robustness score (te_hat + |orc_hat|) for G1 and G2.

  Plot 4 (plot4): Architecture comparison bar chart.
    - Inputs: experiment3_results.csv.
    - Two panels: mean clean ROC-AUC by group (G1–G5) and mean RD_max by group.
    - Groups: Proxy-selected (G1), Uniform (G2), Dense CfC (G3), Random sparse (G4), NCP (G5).
    - Error bars: 95% bootstrap CI (1000 bootstrap samples).

11.8 Results directory layout
  The unified experiment runner writes to:
    results/<paradigm>/<dataset>/<model_short_id>/CrossSessionEvaluation/<seed>/<subject>/<session>/test_perturb/*.csv
  Model short_id is a hash of the model name for path shortening. CSV files contain columns: noise_type, intensity, corrupted_roc_auc, clean_roc_auc (or clean_score).

11.9 Known implementation notes
  - Experiment 1 uses trial index as the graph seed for build_plain_ws_flex, but stores graph_seed from the parameter sampler. If reconstructing from stored (k, p, graph_seed), the graph may differ from the one used for proxy computation. This is a known inconsistency; the stored proxy values are correct for the graph that was actually built.
  - G2 uniform architectures are created with te_hat and orc_hat as NaN initially; Experiment 3 computes them on-the-fly from the graph when needed for plotting.
